== Gamma ended by itself (no kill needed) after ~5-8s, exit=0 ==

FIRST REAL WALL (reproduced 3/3 times, 2026-09-16): while building the
default scene, mainCallback tries to bring up an embedded Netscape
ActiveX control (Netscape.mainCallback -> NSProtocolHandler ->
IUnknown.init) - a COM/ActiveX mechanism exclusive to Windows, with no
equivalent in the mock. IUnknown.init(IUnknown.java:15) opens with
Debug.dAssert(false) (the "not implemented" marker of the 90s code
itself, the same pattern as the dAssert of WorldServer.state_XMIT_SI
documented in README.md) and throws AssertionException (unchecked). It
propagates up to Netscape.mainCallback and from there to IUnknown.Release,
which in turn throws OLEInvalidObjectException while releasing an object
that was never initialized - both exceptions are printed (try/catch with
printStackTrace in the client's own code, NOT in the mock) and the
"Gamma Main" thread stays alive. The process ends by itself with exit 0
a few seconds later with no more console output: no Frame window is
visible at any point of the log (not a single "GammaFrame"/"setVisible"
before this point), so when "Gamma Main" finishes its mainCallback
iteration and no AWT window is shown holding the JVM, the process exits
cleanly as soon as the real "main" thread (blocked in Object.wait, see
below) is released.

Honest scope: this assert does NOT kill the main loop (it is caught with
try/catch in Netscape.java itself, real 2004 code, not an artefact of the
mock) and does NOT prevent the rest of the start (tables, avatar, room,
cache) from having happened correctly before. It is a real but bounded
wall: any feature that depends on that Netscape/ActiveX control (HTML
content embedded in the console, typical of the 2004 UI) will never work
through the mock without re-implementing that whole subsystem - it is not
a candidate for a mock fix (there is no "real value" to return: it is a
structural absence of COM on macOS/Linux, not a getter with a native value
to discover), documented as it is.
